014  | 1,453,018  | 706,821  | 141,622  | 264,852  | 339,637  | 2  | 83  | 14,784,074  | 9.8\nL376: 2015  | 1,524,829  | 742,939  | 143,282  | 273,423  | 365,076  | 2  | 107  | 15,473,741  | 9.9\nL377: 2016  | 1,580,923  | 768,633  | 142,703  | 280,512  | 388,974  | 4  | 97  | 15,887,651  | 10.0\nL378: 2017  | 1,643,643  | 798,722  | 142,740  | 293,349  | 408,740  | 6  | 86  | 16,662,756  | 9.9\nL379: 2018  | 1,719,420  | 844,924  | 143,656  | 303,031  | 427,698  | 7  | 103  | 17,528,192  | 9.8\nL380: 2019  | 1,833,610  | 902,833  | 145,049  | 322,848  | 462,776  | 13  | 92  | 18,363,160  | 10.0\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLabor Market Activity of Blacks in the United States : Spotlight on Statistics: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (https://www.bls.gov/spotlight/2020/)\nciteturn68view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn37search3\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 576\nL187: ## Blacks tend to be younger than the overall population\nL188: \nL189: The Black or African American population tends to be younger in comparison to the overall population age 16 and older. (Population refers to the civilian noninstitutional population age 16 and older.) In 2019, about 69 percent of the Black population was age 16 to 54. By comparison, about 63 percent of the overall population was in the same age range.\nL190: The Black population is also slightly less likely to be made up of men than the overall population. In 2019, Black men made up about 46 percent of the Black population; men made up about 48 percent of the overall population.\nL191: \nL192: View Chart Data\nL193: Percent distribution of the population by educational attainment, 25 years and older, 2019 annual averages\nL194: Educational attainment  | Black or African American  | Total\nL195: --- | --- | ---\nL196: Total  | 100.0%  | 100.0%\nL197: Advanced degree  | 9.7  | 13.4\nL198: Bachelor's degree  | 16.9  | 22.6\nL199: Associate degree  | 10.5  | 10.3\nL200: Some college, no degree  | 18.8  | 15.7\nL201: High school graduates, no college  | 33.0  | 28.2\nL202: Less than a high school diploma  | 11.1  | 9.8\nL203: ## Over a quarter of the Black population has a bachelor’s degree or higher\nL204: \nL205: In 2019, about 27 percent of the Black population age 25 and older had attained a bachelor’s degree or higher, compared with 36 percent of the overall population. Although educational attainment among Blacks has increased over time, 44 percent of the Black population age 25 and older had a high school diploma or less in 2019, compared with 38 percent of the overall population.\nL206: \nL207: View Chart Data\nL208:  Labor force participation rates, 1972–2019 annual averages\nL209: Year  | Black or African\nL210: American total  | Black or African\nL211: American men  | Black or African\nL212: American women  |   Total    | Total men  | Total women\nL213: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL214: 1972  | 59.9%  | 73.6%  | 48.7%  | 60.4%  | 78.9%  | 43.9%\nL215: 1973  | 60.2  | 73.4  | 49.3  | 60.8  | 78.8  | 44.7\nL216: 1974  | 59.8  | 72.9  | 49.0  | 61.3  | 78.7  | 45.7\nL217: 1975  | 58.8  | 70.9  | 48.8  | 61.2  | 77.9  | 46.3\nL218: 1976  | 59.0  | 70.0  | 49.8  | 61.6  | 77.5  | 47.3\nL219: 1977  | 59.8  | 70.6  | 50.8  | 62.3  | 77.7  | 48.4\nL220: 1978  | 61.5  | 71.5  | 53.1  | 63.2  | 77.9  | 50.0\nL221: 1979  | 61.4  | 71.3  | 53.1  | 63.7  | 77.8  | 50.9\nL222: 1980  | 61.0  | 70.3  | 53.1  | 63.8  | 77.4  | 51.5\nL223: 1981  | 60.8  | 70.0  | 53.5  | 63.9  | 77.0  | 52.1\nL224: 1982  | 61.0  | 70.1  | 53.7  | 64.0  | 76.6  | 52.6\nL225: 1983  | 61.5  | 70.6  | 54.2  | 64.0  | 76.4  | 52.9\nL226: 1984  | 62.2  | 70.8  | 55.2  | 64.4  | 76.4  | 53.6\nL227: 1985  | 62.9  | 70.8  | 56.5  | 64.8  | 76.3  | 54.5\nL228: 1986  | 63.3  | 71.2  | 56.9  | 65.3  | 76.3  | 55.3\nL229: 1987  | 63.8  | 71.1  | 58.0  | 65.6  | 76.2  | 56.0\nL230: 1988  | 63.8  | 71.0